def lcs_length(a, b):
    n, m = len(a), len(b)
    dp = [[0] * (m + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]
    for i in range(1, n + 1):
        for j in range(1, m + 1):
            if a[i - 1] == b[j - 1]:
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1
            else:
                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])
    return dp[n][m]
```

Row 0 and column 0 stay all zeros — they represent "zero characters of the other string," so the LCS of anything against an empty prefix is 0. This empty-prefix row and column is what makes the diagonal and neighbor lookups always valid, even at `i = 1` or `j = 1`.
